{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 6, :comments ["<b>And to the sons of the concubines.</b> Rashi's commentary says it is written deficient etc. It is difficult according to Rashi's commentary, for it is plene in precise scrolls — and they are Hagar and Keturah." "<b>Abraham gave gifts.</b> Our Rabbis interpreted there (Sanhedrin 91a): 'in the name of impurity' — he handed over to them the name for swearing demons, that they would be able to mention the Name while the body is in a state of impurity and it would not harm them. And one may say: far be it — that a righteous man such as Abraham would teach any holy Name to the wicked! Rather, he handed over to them a name for swearing demons by their masters appointed over them, that they would do for them whatever they wished; and it is called 'the name of impurity,' just as we learned (Shabbat 67a): one who sleeps in a graveyard, so that a spirit of impurity rest upon him. And a proof for this matter: <b>מתנת</b> is written defective (without vav), and its numerical value equals 'למדם להשביע השדים' (he taught them to swear demons)." "<b>And he sent them away from Isaac his son while he was still.</b> Still living — so that they would not take hold of the inheritance together with Isaac." "<b>To the land of the East.</b> To take hold there of the inheritance that fell to him from his fathers. And these are the gifts that he gave them — for the land of Aram is in the east, as it is written (Numbers 23:7), 'ארם מקדם' (Aram from the East). And furthermore, it is written concerning the son of Nahor, Abraham's brother (Genesis 22:21): 'את עוץ בכורו' (Uz his firstborn), and Job was from the land of Uz. And concerning Job it is written (Job 1:3): 'והוא גדול מכל בני קדם' (and he was the greatest of all the people of the East). It follows that קדם and ארם are one and the same."], :book :genesis, :voice :chizkuni, :lang :en, :chapter 25, :notes {0 "The abbreviation חז״ק has been resolved as חזק (it is difficult), following the standard critical idiom; the text appears to mark the name Rashi with reverent abbreviation.", 2 "The lemma ends at the suspensive בעודנו, and the comment supplies 'living' to complete the sense."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T15:05:52.474038328Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}